From 49cdc8b6a152ffa670d26affcab7fbe5e238aeba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PePe Amengual <2208324+jamengual@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 15:17:22 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 15/16] kubelet: in-place pod-level resize on FreeBSD; keep the
 pod jail limit in step

Pod-level limits are rctl rules on the pod jail. The kubelet decides
whether to update them (UpdatePodSandboxResources) by comparing
GetPodCgroupConfig with the pod's desired config, and the FreeBSD pod
container manager returned the desired config, so pod-level limits were
never updated. That also left the pod jail at the sum of the containers'
limits at creation: growing a container beyond it got the container killed
by the stale pod limit.

The pod container manager now reports what was applied to the pod jail:
recorded at sandbox creation and by SetPodCgroupConfig (called after the
runtime accepted the update); unknown values (e.g. after a kubelet
restart) are reported as unlimited, so the limits are sent again, which is
idempotent. Allow in-place pod-level resize when the kernel enforces jail
rules on child jails (kern.features.rctl_jail_hierarchy); the pod-level
allocation tests now run on such kernels. Tests.
---
